# Faraday's inittab, with the app in a loop. The app
# exits 75 when it locks, and a fresh process starts with nothing of the
# old one's memory (docs/SECURITY-MODEL.md "Stateless"); any other exit ends the loop, and init
# restarts the computer when the app's last status was 76 (Restart on the
# power sheet) and powers off otherwise. rcS mounts /run, a RAM filesystem
# of its own, and leaves the root filesystem read-only, so this inittab
# and the app it starts again stay as the image made them; /run/faraday
# holds the Inbox and Outbox between processes, readable by the app's
# user alone. rcS
# runs the screen script (what it found is in /run/faraday-screen/args,
# read after /etc/faraday/args), starts the grant helper and the disk process (docs/SECURITY-MODEL.md "When root is used").
# The app starts in a session of its own with its input from /dev/null, so it
# has no controlling terminal: a Ctrl-Z or Ctrl-C arriving on a serial port
# (the console's line discipline turns it into a signal for the terminal's
# foreground group) reaches nothing of the app's.
::sysinit:/etc/init.d/rcS
::sysinit:/bin/sh -c 'mkdir -p /run/faraday && chown faraday:faraday /run/faraday && chmod 0700 /run/faraday'
::wait:/bin/sh -c 'while /usr/bin/setsid /bin/setpriv --nnp /usr/bin/setuidgid faraday /usr/bin/faraday $(cat /etc/faraday/args /run/faraday-screen/args 2>/dev/null) </dev/null; code=$?; [ $code -eq 75 ]; do :; done; : > /run/faraday-disk-stop; /usr/bin/killall faraday-disk 2>/dev/null; sync; echo 3 > /proc/sys/vm/drop_caches; if [ $code -eq 76 ]; then /sbin/reboot -f; else /sbin/poweroff -f; fi'
::wait:/sbin/poweroff -f
